    "id": "f4c3d005ca2204b23489855988175ecd6f994e48",
    "message": "Retry macOS release uploads and preserve native test crash evidence\n\nUse case: A green PR should not leave master failures unrecoverable or\nimpossible to diagnose. Master run 39012301248 failed an artifact upload\nwith ENOTFOUND and a separate Vectorscan child with empty captured output.\nThe PR head, tested merge result, and master have identical source trees.\n\nRoot cause: Final artifact uploads are publish-event-only and had no\nworkflow-level retry. The child harness hid its collected exit code behind\na fatal exit-status assertion, and collection restore lacked phase logs.\nThe original child crash itself remains unexplained, not fixed or suppressed.\n\nHow to fix: Share a single bounded macOS release-upload retry, preserving\npayloads, publication gates, retention, and fatal retry failure. Require\nfault-injection contracts in early preflight and retain artifact ancestry\nlint coverage. Record terminal child state and flushed startup/restore\nphases; upload narrow crash reports and CTest logs only after test failure.\nDo not retry tests, skip sanitizer coverage, or weaken ci-gate.\n\nHow to verify: Complete scripts/run_ci_quality.py passed all six lints and\n2229 tests (8 skipped). Focused upload contracts (13), existing preflight\nregressions (16), diagnostic contracts (8), and actionlint passed. Exact\nQt 6.9.3/Xcode 26.6 TSan vectorscan CTest passed 14 cases/68 assertions;\n20 collection children and 100 instrumented Qt launch probes passed.\nAn earlier concurrent quality run hit an unchanged efsw fixture's 2-second\nGit deadline; isolated tests and the complete serial rerun passed without\nchanging its deadline. Hosted crash collection and outage recovery remain\ncovered by offline contracts, not a reproduced hosted failure.\n\nRefs #78\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Code <noreply@anthropic.com>",
